==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 08 NOTES — netcat Part 1
Date: 06-10-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- nc -zv tests if a port is open without actually sending/exchanging real data (-z = scan only)
- Banner grabbing: just connecting (nc -v host port) often shows what a service announces itself as
- A raw HTTP request can be sent manually through netcat using printf piped into nc - no browser needed
- nc -lvnp <port> turns your own machine into a listener (a basic server) on that port
- netcat works both ways: as a client (connecting out) and as a server (listening for connections in)
- Listeners should ONLY ever run on your own machine or your own isolated lab network

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] nc -zv <host> <port>  (single port and a range)
[ ] nc -v <host> 22  (SSH banner grab)
[ ] printf ... | nc -v <host> 80  (manual HTTP request)
[ ] nc -lvnp 4444  (local listener) + nc -v 127.0.0.1 4444  (connecting to it)

paste your real output here :
$ nc -zv scanme.nmap.org 22
> 

$ nc -v scanme.nmap.org 22  (banner)
> 

$ printf "GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: scanme.nmap.org\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n" | nc -v scanme.nmap.org 80
> 


